name{csc}⁡a=-3,$ then $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-a)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-a) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡a \\\\  & =-(-3) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a710f9-fed1-5fdb-b4c0-fd3ae2d4d8a4" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c7814cb-8a14-543c-b191-090e1d7ec186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272638} {:answer/id #uuid "912d0bf4-9153-5eaf-8129-ed93f063eca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273648} {:answer/id #uuid "979f59c9-f61f-5d15-890f-5f7e912c17f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273701} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c841e0-0d84-5ffb-afef-0ed443247d86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274456} {:answer/id #uuid "fb39b869-1aff-5ccd-a39a-e66bfc83a392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9c841e0-0d84-5ffb-afef-0ed443247d86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274456} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca6a7db3-5479-511f-aa28-280da1724856" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-fiel