operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+22\\pi ) = -\\sqrt{10}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a710f9-fed1-5fdb-b4c0-fd3ae2d4d8a4" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "721caa62-b29d-5096-9d5e-7aaddee2a6dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515873} {:answer/id #uuid "89198e2a-a365-5671-94e2-73027b795541" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515881} {:answer/id #uuid "95eb68af-17ae-5dd7-99ea-3d75a46e3f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 + 6 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515884} {:answer/id #uuid "acf7d1ff-7244-5ce0-97b0-3efdcbe014f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12 + 6 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515890} {:answer/id #uuid "c017d22d-abc6-5cdb-aa6f-3e41bdbf51c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89198e2a-a365-5671-94e2-73027b795541" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515881} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd8ce3d3-ba67-514e-b8d0-0db74bb5bb84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4265ea6a-ebc1-5dc6-8794-cc8d1c049c85" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=12,$ then $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+6\\pi )=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+6\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+6\\pi ) = -12.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a710f9-fed1-5fdb-b4c0-fd3ae2d4d8a4" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cface83-381b-58c3-895b-8f62b382825c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515850} {:answer/id #uuid "7926303a-e2b6-5159-985f-0ad46e76f612" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515875} {:answer/id #uuid "9fee32d0-78da-51c3-a53c-1e137107d56d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{37}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515886} {:answer/id #uuid "a238cdeb-c29f-50a9-a436-52e309bee941" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{37}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515887} {:answer/id #uuid "cf1d0da7-26e6-50bb-81f0-8728aeea07aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{39}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cface83-381b-58c3-895b-8f62b382825c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515850} :answer-field/id #uuid "a920c61e-b853-5722-89c4-a929b87344b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "449a125e-7ab5-525b-be8a-783acbe8eade" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{5}{3},$ then $2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+10\\pi )+9=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+10\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 5) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+10\\pi )+9 & =2(-\\frac{5}{3})+9 \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{3}+9 \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515861 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a710f9-fed1-5fdb-b4c0-fd3ae2d4d8a4" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16b4b7ea-ccfa-5fb0-ac66-c92bcd973c7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515848} {:answer/id #uuid "2775cb69-96e7-556d-b785-b3056e5b7e2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515851} {:answer/id #uuid "3f5fcdf6-fbd8-5749-bbd7-802c0901b629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515856} {:answer/id #uuid "50987994-0587-5d87-922a-ee58b685e2f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515862} {:answer/id #uuid "de7497a8-2b7d-53bf-a87a-8f8544ae3a2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50987994-0587-5d87-922a-ee58b685e2f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515862} :answer-field/id #uuid "80a6f0bf-7da6-5689-b4c3-2e4cae8cc4de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48e46b45-79fc-5bed-bcc8-55e28b01f005" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡a=-\\sqrt{2},$ then $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-a)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-a) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡a \\\\  & =-(-\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a710f9-fed1-5fdb-b4c0-fd3ae2d4d8a4" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31a88af4-8384-5639-bf7b-0376a7a24afc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515854} {:answer/id #uuid "5516ea7d-7220-58d4-b797-024578518fed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7 + 4 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515865} {:answer/id #uuid "577aa6b7-a69d-5921-b72c-b91c9cab4ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7 + 4 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515866} {:answer/id #uuid "bd78f7fa-ec3f-5a63-907f-e827a554c131" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515897} {:answer/id #uuid "caea55ef-c921-544c-864c-ea8d4097c6d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "caea55ef-c921-544c-864c-ea8d4097c6d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515901} :answer-field/id #uuid "865f6d4b-dfa1-5597-b564-d795f52df4f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54e02bf2-6dc6-5609-a00e-d3a86b2c2438" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-7,$ then $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+4\\pi )=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+4\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\  & =-(-7) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+4\\pi ) = 7.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a710f9-fed1-5fdb-b4c0-fd3ae2d4d8a4" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eef5d31-5d96-5bc6-8af2-a30183e46023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515845} {:answer/id #uuid "41effadf-0a43-5978-ad71-bdf0a0c1470c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515857} {:answer/id #uuid "63d42323-09c0-5218-bf82-c9857786f858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515869} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a510a4-f582-52ba-8dc7-f29bd1140043" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515910} {:answer/id #uuid "fc4ff9b0-0b51-5910-b67a-05e20d71bb9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0eef5d31-5d96-5bc6-8af2-a30183e46023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515845} :answer-field/id #uuid "542e36da-f3fe-5d80-8f83-fb94f16ccc90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "71b09ca0-a60f-5568-b769-d54083824c84" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=7,$ then $3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+22\\pi )+3=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+22\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 11) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+22\\pi )+3 & =3(-7)+3 \\\\  & =-21+3 \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a710f9-fed1-5fdb-b4c0-fd3ae2d4d8a4" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a951cad-77de-5257-91b1-dfa95ad514db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515849} {:answer/id #uuid "6efcd8c1-8f9f-55d2-a6d3-243401e4bb8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515871} {:answer/id #uuid "bb6249b4-73c3-5417-b58d-84aa385974bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515896} {:answer/id #uuid "da6363ea-ebbf-5668-b296-4a4e976c5228" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515903} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc8b265-6695-5580-b774-97f95f82acb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da6363ea-ebbf-5668-b296-4a4e976c5228" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515903} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2cf3c1e-7afc-5e30-b7cb-f41ea44bfbe5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93ab5619-2754-55a0-8f2a-c912609fd8ff" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡a=\\frac{5}{3},$ then $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-a)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-a) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡a \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a710f9-fed1-5fdb-b4c0-fd3ae2d4d8a4" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1436ae2b-ee6b-5f93-b74b-0d51cacac37c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515847} {:answer/id #uuid "310028a4-e941-57c6-90b6-15e12116a8da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515853} {:answer/id #uuid "5a29c973-a7ca-5a66-8f0d-9d2e057cc260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8 \\pi -\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515867} {:answer/id #uuid "6a1d29b7-d01f-512a-a792-6e043ffc2d40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8 \\pi + \\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515870} {:answer/id #uuid "80417651-3504-5aa8-b9f5-6a7185bd0a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80417651-3504-5aa8-b9f5-6a7185bd0a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515877} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef4fe93f-ed73-547c-bcbf-c277f14d0cb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b147ede7-d5a6-5e9c-9f7e-acaf3b16b20d" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\sqrt{13},$ then $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-8\\pi )=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-8\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-2\\pi \\cdot 4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\  & =-(-\\sqrt{13}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-8\\pi ) = \\sqrt{13}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515895 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a710f9-fed1-5fdb-b4c0-fd3ae2d4d8a4" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75800c1e-cf7f-58e0-bdd6-02c7cd6d7611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12 \\pi + \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515874} {:answer/id #uuid "7bffa902-719c-5251-9cad-683d7e126b6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12 \\pi -\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515876} {:answer/id #uuid "8378f689-dcf6-5222-a684-e0b21c2ab31e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515879} {:answer/id #uuid "91c3e9fc-a09c-5a8b-9d9a-7ec6c2f745a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515882} {:answer/id #uuid "a427e028-3460-5e82-b0dd-82323ad78e56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8378f689-dcf6-5222-a684-e0b21c2ab31e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515879} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaac6009-099e-5751-9fa1-d69ece571a8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8c12ef4-0761-5d6c-86d0-727ff0743631" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3},$ then $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-12\\pi )=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-12\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-2\\pi \\cdot 6) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\  & =-(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-12\\pi ) = \\sqrt{3}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Cosecant Function"}]}
